Here's an example implementation of a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text:
```
#include <string>
#include <regex>
#include <iostream>

std::string convertMarkdownToHtml(std::string markdown)
{
    // Define a regular expression that matches markdown formatting
    // symbols. This regex will be used to replace the formatting
    // symbols with HTML tags.
    std::regex markdownRegex("(\\s)*([*\\\\]\\*+\\\\2|\\b\\w+\\b|\\S)\\s*");

    // Use a replace_callback function to replace the formatting
    // symbols with HTML tags.
    return std::regex_replace(markdown, markdownRegex,
        std::bind(std::function<std::string(const std::string&)>,
                  std::ref(convertMarkdownToHtml), std::placeholders::_1));
}

// Define the convertMarkdownToHtml function inline
template <typename F>
F convertMarkdownToHtml(std::string markdown) {
    // Define a regular expression that matches markdown formatting
    // symbols. This regex will be used to replace the formatting
    // symbols with HTML tags.
    std::regex markdownRegex("(\\s)*([*\\\\]\\*+\\\\2|\\b\\w+\\b|\\S)\\s*");

    // Use a replace_callback function to replace the formatting
    // symbols with HTML tags.
    return std::regex_replace(markdown, markdownRegex,
        std::bind(std::function<std::string(const std::string&)>,
                  std::ref(convertMarkdownToHtml), std::placeholders::_1));
}
```
This implementation uses the `std::regex` class to define a regular expression that matches markdown formatting symbols such as `*` and `_`. It then uses the `std::regex_replace` function to replace the formatting symbols with HTML tags. The `std::bind` and `std::function` classes are used to create a `std::function` object that can be passed to the `std::regex_replace` function's third argument, which is a callback function that takes a match object as its argument and returns a replacement string. In this case, the `convertMarkdownToHtml` function is called recursively to convert any nested markdown formatting symbols. The `std::placeholders::_1` argument is used to specify that the first argument to the callback function is the match object.

