Here is how you can implement Timsort in Lean 4:

```c++
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>

void timsort(std::vector<int>& arr) {
    // define a helper function to compare two elements
    auto comp = [](int a, int b) { return a > b; };

    timsort_helper(arr, 0, arr.size() - 1, comp);
}

void timsort_helper(std::vector<int>& arr, int left, int right, std::function<bool(int, int)> comp) {
    if (left < right) {
        int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
        // partition the array around the middle element
        int i = left, j = right;
        while (i < j) {
            // loop until the elements on the left side are smaller than the middle element
            while (i < middle && !comp(arr[i], arr[middle])) {
                i++;
            }
            // loop until the elements on the right side are larger than the middle element
            while (j > middle && comp(arr[middle], arr[j])) {
                j--;
            }
            if (i < j) {
                // swap the elements on the left and right sides
                std::swap(arr[i], arr[j]);
            }
        }
        // recursively sort the left and right halves
        timsort_helper(arr, left, j - 1, comp);
        timsort_helper(arr, j + 1, right, comp);
    }
}
```

Explanation of the code:

- The timsort function takes an array of integers as input and calls the timsort_helper function to perform the sorting.
- The timsort_helper function is the main sorting function. It takes the array, the left and right indices, and a comparison function as input, and recursively sorts the left and right halves of the array.
- The comparison function is a lambda function that takes two integers as input and returns whether the first integer is greater than the second.
- The partition step is the heart of the Timsort algorithm. It works by iteratively swapping elements until the left side of the middle element is sorted in ascending order, and the right side is sorted in descending order.
- The i and j variables are used to keep track of the indices of the elements being compared. The loop continues until both the left and right sides are sorted.
- Once the partition step is complete, the left and right halves are recursively sorted using the timsort_helper function.

