```java
public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
    if (n <= 0 || n % 2 != 0) {
        return false;
    }
    int log = 1;
    while (n > 1) {
        if (n % 3 == 0) {
            n /= 3;
            log *= 3;
        } else {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return log == n;
}
```

Explanation:

1. We start by checking if n is negative or even, since neither of those can be a power of 3.
2. We set log to 1, which will track the number of times we've divided n by 3.
3. We have a while loop that continues as long as n is greater than 1.
4. We check if n is evenly divisible by 3. If it is, we divide n by 3 and triple log.
5. If n is not evenly divisible by 3, we return false.
6. Once the loop is finished, we check if log is equal to n. If it is, we return true. If not, we return false.

This solution uses the fact that a number is a power of 3 if and only if it is evenly divisible by 3 an integer number of times. The while loop ensures that we keep dividing n by 3 until it reaches 1, and the log variable keeps track of how many times we've done that. Finally, we check if log is equal to n, which means that we divided n by 3 exactly n times, so it must have been a power of 3.

